Here are strong, ready-to-rehearse answers for each question — written in first person so you can adapt them directly. I've kept
  them structured (framework/trade-off/example) so they're easy to recall under pressure.

  1. Architecture & Strategy

  Q: Design a framework from scratch for microservices + web UI + mobile — top 5 decisions?

  ▎ "First, I decouple layers: a core engine (driver management, config, reporting) separate from test logic, separate from page
  ▎ objects/API clients. Second, I pick abstraction over tool lock-in — wrap Selenium/Playwright calls behind an interface so the
  ▎ framework survives a tool migration. Third, I separate execution concerns from business logic — BDD feature files describe
  ▎ behavior, step definitions call reusable action/service classes, not raw driver calls. Fourth, I design for parallel-first
  ▎ execution from day one — thread-safe driver instances, isolated test data per thread, not retrofitted later. Fifth, I build in
  ▎ observability early — structured logging, screenshots/video on failure, and CI-integrated reporting (Allure/ExtentReports) so
  ▎ failures are triageable without re-running locally."

  Q: Selenium 4 vs Playwright — how do you decide, and what are the real trade-offs?

  ▎ "Playwright wins on speed, auto-waiting, and built-in network interception/mocking — that eliminates a huge class of flaky-wait
  ▎ issues Selenium teams fight constantly. It also has native multi-tab/multi-context support, which is painful in Selenium.
  ▎ Selenium 4 wins on ecosystem maturity, broader real-device/browser grid support (especially legacy browser requirements, some
  ▎ enterprise environments), and larger community/tooling for BDD integration. My decision driver is: if it's a greenfield project
  ▎ with modern browser targets and no legacy browser requirement, Playwright + TypeScript is my default for speed of both execution
  ▎ and authoring. If there's an existing large Selenium investment, or a need for RemoteWebDriver/Grid-based device farms with less
  ▎ common browser/OS combos, I stay with Selenium 4 and invest in Grid 4's improved architecture (Selenium Manager, better session
  ▎ queuing) rather than a risky rewrite."

  Q: Hybrid framework supporting UI, API, DB validation without becoming unmaintainable?

  ▎ "I layer it: a common 'core' module (config, logging, reporting, environment management) shared by all test types. Then separate
  ▎ modules for UI (page objects/POM), API (REST Assured request specs/response POJOs), and DB (repository/DAO pattern for query
  ▎ validation) — each with their own utilities but sharing the same test runner, data providers, and reporting pipeline. The key
  ▎ discipline is: business/test logic never talks to Selenium or REST Assured directly — it goes through a service layer. That's
  ▎ what keeps it from becoming a monolith; each layer can evolve or be replaced independently."

  Q: Framework tool-agnostic to swap Selenium for Playwright later?

  ▎ "I introduce a driver-abstraction interface — my step definitions and page objects call methods like click(), type(),
  ▎ waitForElement() from an interface, and the concrete implementation (SeleniumDriverAdapter vs PlaywrightDriverAdapter) is
  ▎ injected via config/factory pattern. This is more upfront work, but it's paid off in real migrations — I've done partial
  ▎ migrations this way without touching a single test scenario, only the adapter layer."

  Q: Test data management strategy across environments?

  ▎ "Three principles: test data should be self-contained per test (create-then-cleanup, not shared fixtures, to enable parallel
  ▎ execution), environment-specific config should be externalized (YAML/properties per env, injected via CI variables, never
  ▎ hardcoded), and for data that's expensive to generate, I use API-based setup/teardown instead of UI-based, since it's faster and
  ▎ more reliable. For complex scenarios I also maintain synthetic data builders (builder pattern) rather than static JSON fixtures,
  ▎ so tests can vary data without duplicating files."

  2. Technical Depth

  Q: Scaling Selenium Grid 4 — configuration and issues hit?

  ▎ "Grid 4's key improvement is the distributed architecture — Router, Distributor, Session Map, Node — which I run either via
  ▎ Docker Compose for on-prem scaling or Kubernetes for cloud elasticity. I configure node capacity based on CPU/memory per browser
  ▎ type, since headless Chrome vs Firefox has very different resource footprints. Real issues I've hit: session leaks when tests
  ▎ crash mid-run without releasing the driver (fixed with hard timeouts and cleanup hooks), node registration flakiness under high
  ▎ load (fixed with health-check retries), and browser version drift between nodes causing inconsistent failures (fixed by pinning
  ▎ browser/driver versions in the node Docker image and using Selenium Manager)."

  Q: Systematic flaky test handling — beyond retries?

  ▎ "Retries mask problems; I use them only as a safety net, capped at 1 retry, with retry occurrences logged and tracked. My real
  ▎ process: tag every flaky failure, categorize root cause (timing/sync issue, test data collision, environment instability, genuine
  ▎ app bug, third-party dependency), and track a flaky-test dashboard by category. Sync issues get fixed with explicit
  ▎ waits/Playwright auto-wait instead of sleeps. Data collision gets fixed with better test isolation. If a test flakes 3+ times
  ▎ with no clear fix, it gets quarantined out of the main suite and assigned an owner with an SLA to fix or delete it — flaky tests
  ▎ that just get ignored destroy trust in the whole suite."

  Q: Preventing step-definition sprawl/duplicate glue code in BDD at scale?

  ▎ "I enforce a shared step-definition library reviewed like production code, with a naming convention and a searchable glossary so
  ▎ engineers check before writing a new step. I also push teams toward parameterized, reusable steps (e.g., I click on "<element>"
  ▎ rather than one step per button) and keep step definitions thin — they call service/page-object methods, never contain business
  ▎ logic themselves. Periodic 'step definition audits' in code review catch duplication before it multiplies across 10+
  ▎ contributors."

  Q: Structuring REST Assured for large API surfaces?

  ▎ "I build reusable request specifications (RequestSpecBuilder) per service/base-URL, POJOs for request/response with schema
  ▎ validation (JSON Schema or matching POJO deserialization), and a token-management utility that handles auth once per suite run
  ▎ (cached token, refreshed on expiry) rather than per-test login calls. For chained API calls (e.g., create → update → verify), I
  ▎ use a fluent builder pattern so test code reads top-to-bottom instead of nested callback logic. Response validation is layered:
  ▎ status code, schema, and business-rule assertions are separate reusable methods, not duplicated per test."

  Q: Synchronization/waits — Selenium 4 vs Playwright, common mistakes?

  ▎ "Playwright auto-waits for actionability (visible, stable, enabled) before interacting, which eliminates most explicit-wait bugs
  ▎ by default. Selenium 4 still requires explicit WebDriverWait with ExpectedConditions — the most common mistake I see is teams
  ▎ mixing implicit and explicit waits, which causes unpredictable timeout behavior, or using Thread.sleep() as a crutch, which is
  ▎ both slow and still flaky. My rule: no sleeps, ever; explicit waits on specific conditions only, and for Playwright, trust
  ▎ auto-wait but still add explicit waits for custom conditions like network idle or a specific API response."

  3. CI/CD & DevOps Integration

  Q: GitHub Actions vs Jenkins pipeline structuring?

  ▎ "In GitHub Actions, I use a matrix strategy to parallelize across browsers/shards, with reusable composite actions for common
  ▎ steps (checkout, setup, dependency cache) to avoid duplicating YAML across workflows. In Jenkins, I've built declarative
  ▎ pipelines with parallel stages and shared libraries for the same reusability goal — the concepts map directly, just different
  ▎ syntax. My decision on which to use often came down to existing org infrastructure rather than a technical limitation of either
  ▎ tool. Both get artifact publishing (reports, screenshots) and Slack/Teams notification on failure wired in as standard."

  Q: What runs on PR vs nightly vs pre-release?

  ▎ "PR-triggered: fast smoke suite (critical path, <10 min) to give quick feedback and not block developer flow. Nightly: full
  ▎ regression suite across all supported browsers/environments, since it can tolerate longer runtime. Pre-release: full regression +
  ▎ any manual exploratory sign-off + performance/security scan gates if applicable. This tiering is the single biggest lever for
  ▎ keeping CI fast without sacrificing coverage confidence."

  Q: Handling test reporting/failure triage to avoid alert fatigue?

  ▎ "Every CI failure needs a categorized reason before anyone even looks at it manually — I integrate reporting tools
  ▎ (Allure/ExtentReports) with automatic failure classification tagging (assertion failure vs environment/infra failure vs timeout)
  ▎ so the team can filter noise immediately. I also enforce a 'red build owner' rotation — someone is always accountable for
  ▎ triaging a failure within a defined SLA, so failures don't just get dismissed as 'probably flaky.'"

  Q: Containerizing test execution for consistent CI environments?

  ▎ "I containerize the test runner and, where applicable, the Selenium Grid nodes, pinning browser and driver versions in the Docker
  ▎ image so CI runs are reproducible regardless of the underlying CI host. This also lets me run the exact same suite locally as in
  ▎ CI, which eliminates the 'works on my machine, fails in CI' class of problems entirely."

  4. Leadership & Influence

  Q: Driving automation adoption when developers push back?

  ▎ "I don't lead with mandates — I lead with data and low-friction entry points. I show the cost of manual regression testing in
  ▎ hours/release, then demonstrate a small automated win in an area developers already feel pain (e.g., a repetitive smoke test they
  ▎ run manually before every PR). I also make contribution easy — clear framework docs, reusable step libraries, pairing sessions —
  ▎ so writing a test isn't a multi-day learning curve. Adoption follows when automation clearly saves them time, not when it's
  ▎ imposed top-down."

  Q: A time a framework decision was wrong in hindsight?

  ▎ "Early in a hybrid framework build, I over-engineered a plugin-based architecture anticipating tool swaps that never happened —
  ▎ it added complexity that slowed every engineer down for a flexibility we used maybe once in two years. I course-corrected by
  ▎ simplifying back toward a more direct architecture and reserving abstraction only for the layers that actually changed
  ▎ (driver/tool layer), not everything speculatively. Lesson: abstract where change is likely, not everywhere 'just in case.'"

  Q: Balancing 'automate everything' pressure against ROI/maintainability?

  ▎ "I frame automation as an investment with a payback period, not a blanket goal. High-value candidates: repetitive regression,
  ▎ high business-risk paths, stable UI/API. Low-value candidates: one-off exploratory scenarios, highly volatile UI still in active
  ▎ design, edge cases better covered by targeted unit/integration tests. I present this as a prioritized backlog with ROI reasoning
  ▎ to stakeholders, rather than either automating everything or refusing outright — that keeps the conversation collaborative
  ▎ instead of adversarial."

  Q: Mentoring engineers to think architecturally, not just script?

  ▎ "I use design reviews before code reviews — before someone writes a new module, we discuss the approach: reusability, failure
  ▎ points, maintenance cost. I also rotate ownership of framework core components (not just test scripts) so engineers experience
  ▎ the pain of maintaining what they design, which builds architectural instinct faster than any lecture would."

  5. Scenario-Based

  Q: Fintech platform, web + mobile + 40+ microservices, 15 SDETs, 3 time zones — strategy?

  ▎ "I'd start with a test pyramid mapped to the architecture: heavy API/contract-level testing directly against the 40+
  ▎ microservices (since UI-level testing every service interaction doesn't scale), a thin UI layer for critical user journeys only,
  ▎ and mobile covered by a shared automation core where possible (e.g., Appium alongside the web framework, reusing API/data
  ▎ layers). Organizationally, with 15 SDETs across 3 time zones, I'd assign domain ownership — each sub-team owns automation for
  ▎ their service domain, but all contribute to a shared core framework governed by an architecture guild to prevent divergence. For
  ▎ fintech specifically, I'd add mandatory security/compliance-focused test suites (auth, PII handling, transaction integrity) as a
  ▎ non-negotiable gate, and ensure test data never touches real financial data — synthetic data generation only. CI would be tiered
  ▎ as described earlier (PR smoke, nightly regression per domain, pre-release full run), with cross-time-zone coverage achieved by a
  ▎ 'follow the sun' triage rotation for red builds."

  Q: 6-hour regression suite blocking releases — reduce to <45 min?

  ▎ "First, I'd profile the suite to find the actual bottleneck — usually it's serial execution, not test count. I'd parallelize
  ▎ aggressively across Grid nodes/containers, since that alone often cuts hours to tens of minutes. Second, I'd apply risk-based
  ▎ test selection — tag tests by criticality and only run full-priority tests on every build, deferring lower-priority/edge-case
  ▎ tests to nightly. Third, I'd push UI-level tests down to API/unit level wherever the same validation doesn't need a browser —
  ▎ every UI test replaced by an equivalent API test is 10x faster. Fourth, I'd eliminate flaky/redundant tests found via the
  ▎ flaky-test tracking process. Combining parallelization + risk-based selection typically gets you there without sacrificing real
  ▎ coverage confidence — I'd present before/after coverage metrics to prove that to stakeholders, not just the speed win."

  6. Behavioral

  Q: Biggest automation framework failure/rewrite — what went wrong, what changed?

  ▎ "On one project, we built a framework tightly coupled to Selenium's WebElement type throughout the codebase, with no abstraction
  ▎ layer. When the team needed to add Playwright for a different project sharing infrastructure, we couldn't reuse anything — a full
  ▎ rewrite was needed. What went wrong: I optimized for shipping speed early on without considering the abstraction cost was cheap
  ▎ early but expensive to retrofit later. Now, on every new framework, I build the driver-abstraction layer from day one, even when
  ▎ there's no immediate multi-tool need — it's cheap insurance that's saved real rewrite cost since."

  Q: Evaluating whether to adopt a new tool vs sticking with what the team knows?

  ▎ "I run a time-boxed spike — pick 2-3 representative real test scenarios (not toy examples) and implement them in both the current
  ▎ and candidate tool, measuring authoring time, execution time/stability, and CI integration effort. I weigh that against team
  ▎ ramp-up cost and existing test-suite migration cost. I only recommend switching when the quantified benefit clearly outweighs the
  ▎ migration cost and ramp-up time — 'newer/faster' alone isn't sufficient justification given 19 years of seeing tool-hype cycles
  ▎ come and go."
